===Etymology 1===
[[File:Runic letter kauna.png|class=skin-invert-image|50px|left|The k-rune ᚲ, an older version of Anglo-Saxon Futhorc letter ᚳ]]
[[File:Rune-Cen.png|class=skin-invert-image|Anglo-Saxon Futhorc letter ᚳ, which was later replaced by Latin ‘c’]] {{der|en|ang|-}} lower case letter {{m|ang|c}}, from 7th century replacement by Latin lower case {{m|la|c}} of the Anglo-Saxon Futhorc letter {{m|mul|ᚳ|tr=c||cen}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{sense|letter name}} {{IPA|en|/siː/}} (usually spelled {{m|en|cee}})
:: {{audio|en|en-uk-c.ogg|a=UK}}
:: {{audio|en|en-us-c.ogg|a=US}}
:: {{rhymes|en|iː|s=1}}
* {{sense|phoneme}} {{IPA|en|/k/|/s/|/tʃ/}}, ...
* Usage notes: In English, c is usually pronounced as /k/ ("crack", "climb", "clone"), sometimes pronounced as /s/ ("cereal", "celebrity", "[[citron]]"), and rarely pronounced as /tʃ/ ("[[ciao]]", "[[cello]]", "[[vermicelli]]"). Sometimes c is pronounced as /tʃ/ due to {{w|List of English words of Italian origin|English words that came from Italian}}. (Italian has a rule that states that [[c#Italian|c before i or e is pronounced as /tʃ/]].[http://www.italymagazine.com/featured-story/how-pronounce-c-italian])

